### What is Spring Boot Actuator?

Spring Boot Actuator is a module that adds production-ready monitoring and management endpoints to your application. You add a single dependency, and Spring Boot auto-configures endpoints for health checks, metrics, environment inspection, bean listing, thread dumps, and log management. These endpoints are essential for running applications in production, especially in Kubernetes.

The key endpoints are:

- `/actuator/health` : reports whether the application is UP or DOWN.
- `/actuator/health/liveness` : Kubernetes liveness probe.
- `/actuator/health/readiness` : Kubernetes readiness probe.
- `/actuator/prometheus` : exports all metrics in Prometheus format.
- `/actuator/loggers` : view and change log levels at runtime.
- `/actuator/info` : application information (version, git commit, etc.).
- `/actuator/env` : shows all configuration properties and their sources.
- `/actuator/beans` : lists all beans in the ApplicationContext.
- `/actuator/threaddump` : captures a thread dump for debugging deadlocks.

```xml
<!-- pom.xml: add Actuator dependency -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
</dependency>

<!-- For Prometheus metrics export -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
    <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```yaml
# application.yml: configure Actuator
management:
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health, prometheus, loggers, info, env
  endpoint:
    health:
      show-details: when-authorized
  health:
    probes:
      enabled: true
```

```text
GET /actuator/health
{
  "status": "UP",
  "components": {
    "db": { "status": "UP" },
    "diskSpace": { "status": "UP" },
    "coreBanking": { "status": "UP", "details": { "provider": "reachable" } }
  }
}
```

### How do you add a custom health check?

A custom `HealthIndicator` checks business-specific dependencies that Spring Boot does not know about. At National Bank of India, Rajesh needs to check whether the core banking system is reachable. If it is down, the service should report itself as not ready to serve traffic.

You implement the `HealthIndicator` interface and return `Health.up()` or `Health.down()` with descriptive details. Spring Boot automatically includes your custom indicator in the `/actuator/health` response.

```java
package com.bank.health;

import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class CoreBankingHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    private final CoreBankingClient coreBankingClient;

    public CoreBankingHealthIndicator(CoreBankingClient coreBankingClient) {
        this.coreBankingClient = coreBankingClient;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        try {
            boolean isReachable = coreBankingClient.ping();
            if (isReachable) {
                return Health.up()
                    .withDetail("provider", "reachable")
                    .withDetail("responseTime", "45ms")
                    .build();
            }
            return Health.down()
                .withDetail("provider", "unreachable")
                .withDetail("reason", "connection refused")
                .build();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            return Health.down(e).build();
        }
    }
}
```

```text
GET /actuator/health
{
  "status": "DOWN",
  "components": {
    "coreBanking": {
      "status": "DOWN",
      "details": {
        "provider": "unreachable",
        "reason": "connection refused"
      }
    },
    "db": { "status": "UP" }
  }
}
```

### What is the difference between liveness and readiness probes?

Kubernetes uses two types of probes to manage pods, and confusing them is a common production mistake. Liveness checks if the JVM is alive and functioning. Readiness checks if the application is ready to accept traffic.

| Aspect | Liveness Probe | Readiness Probe |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Is the JVM alive? | Can the app serve requests? |
| Failure action | Kubernetes restarts the pod | Kubernetes removes pod from load balancer |
| What to check | Internal JVM health only | DB, cache, external services, startup tasks |
| External deps | Never include | Always include |
| Endpoint | `/actuator/health/liveness` | `/actuator/health/readiness` |

Pankaj designed the probes at National Bank of India with a critical rule: never include external dependencies in the liveness probe. If the database goes down, the liveness probe should still pass. A database outage should not cause pod restarts, which would create a cascading failure.

```yaml
# Kubernetes deployment
spec:
  containers:
    - name: account-service
      readinessProbe:
        httpGet:
          path: /actuator/health/readiness
          port: 9092
        initialDelaySeconds: 10
        periodSeconds: 5
      livenessProbe:
        httpGet:
          path: /actuator/health/liveness
          port: 9092
        initialDelaySeconds: 15
        periodSeconds: 10
```

### How do you create custom business metrics?

Micrometer is the metrics facade that integrates with Prometheus. You define custom metrics using Counter (things that only go up, like total transactions), Gauge (values that go up and down, like active connections), and Timer (duration measurements, like request processing time).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Counter;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Timer;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final Counter successCounter;
    private final Counter failureCounter;
    private final Timer transferTimer;

    public TransferService(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.successCounter = Counter.builder("bank.transfers.success")
            .description("Total successful transfers")
            .tag("type", "NEFT")
            .register(registry);

        this.failureCounter = Counter.builder("bank.transfers.failure")
            .description("Total failed transfers")
            .tag("type", "NEFT")
            .register(registry);

        this.transferTimer = Timer.builder("bank.transfers.duration")
            .description("Transfer processing time")
            .register(registry);
    }

    public void transfer(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {
        transferTimer.record(() -> {
            try {
                executeTransfer(fromAccount, toAccount, amount);
                successCounter.increment();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                failureCounter.increment();
                throw e;
            }
        });
    }
}
```

```text
GET /actuator/prometheus

# HELP bank_transfers_success_total Total successful transfers
# TYPE bank_transfers_success_total counter
bank_transfers_success_total{type="NEFT"} 1542.0

# HELP bank_transfers_failure_total Total failed transfers
# TYPE bank_transfers_failure_total counter
bank_transfers_failure_total{type="NEFT"} 3.0

# HELP bank_transfers_duration_seconds Transfer processing time
# TYPE bank_transfers_duration_seconds summary
bank_transfers_duration_seconds_count 1545
bank_transfers_duration_seconds_sum 231.75
```

### How do you run Actuator on a separate port?

In production, Actuator endpoints should not be accessible from the public internet. You run them on a separate management port that is only accessible within the Kubernetes cluster. The main application serves customer traffic on port 8080, while Actuator serves monitoring traffic on port 9092.

```yaml
# application-prod.yml
server:
  port: 8080                        # public traffic

management:
  server:
    port: 9092                       # internal monitoring only
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health, prometheus, loggers, info
      base-path: /actuator
```

```text
Public load balancer:
  http://account-service:8080/api/v1/accounts/SAV2001   <- Priya's request

Internal cluster only:
  http://account-service:9092/actuator/health            <- K8s probe
  http://account-service:9092/actuator/prometheus         <- Prometheus scraper
  http://account-service:9092/actuator/loggers            <- SRE during incident
```

### How do you change log levels at runtime?

The `/actuator/loggers` endpoint lets you change the log level of any package at runtime without restarting the application. This is critical during production incidents when you need DEBUG-level detail for a specific service without restarting the pod and losing the state of the issue.

```bash
# Check current log level for a package
curl http://localhost:9092/actuator/loggers/com.bank.service

# Change to DEBUG for troubleshooting
curl -X POST http://localhost:9092/actuator/loggers/com.bank.service \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"configuredLevel": "DEBUG"}'

# Revert to INFO after the incident
curl -X POST http://localhost:9092/actuator/loggers/com.bank.service \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"configuredLevel": "INFO"}'
```

```text
Before change:
{"configuredLevel": "INFO", "effectiveLevel": "INFO"}

After change:
{"configuredLevel": "DEBUG", "effectiveLevel": "DEBUG"}

Now all log.debug() calls in com.bank.service produce output.
```

### What are common mistakes with Actuator?

The most common mistake is exposing all Actuator endpoints to the public internet. Endpoints like `/env` and `/beans` reveal sensitive configuration details including database credentials and API keys.

```yaml
# WRONG: expose everything to the internet
management:
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: "*"
# /actuator/env shows database passwords, API keys, secrets

# CORRECT: expose only what is needed, on a separate port
management:
  server:
    port: 9092              # internal port only
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health, prometheus, loggers, info
  endpoint:
    health:
      show-details: when-authorized    # require auth for details
    env:
      enabled: false                   # disable entirely
```

Another mistake is including external dependencies in the liveness probe. If the database is unreachable, the pod should be removed from the load balancer (readiness fails), not restarted (liveness fails). Restarting pods during a database outage creates a restart loop that makes recovery harder.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Use `@Timed` annotation from Micrometer to automatically time controller methods without manual Timer code.
- Custom `HealthIndicator` beans are automatically included in the health response. Group them into readiness and liveness using `management.endpoint.health.group.readiness.include` and `management.endpoint.health.group.liveness.include`.
- Spring Boot Admin is a web application that provides a dashboard for monitoring multiple Spring Boot instances. Each application registers with the Admin server, and the dashboard shows health status, metrics, log levels, and thread dumps in a single UI.
- In a microservices architecture, Pankaj configures Prometheus to scrape all services, stores metrics in a time-series database, and visualizes them in Grafana with alerts for SLA breaches.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import io.micrometer.core.annotation.Timed;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/accounts")
public class AccountController {

    private final AccountService accountService;

    public AccountController(AccountService accountService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    @Timed(value = "bank.accounts.getBalance", description = "Time to fetch account balance")
    @GetMapping("/{accountId}/balance")
    public BigDecimal getBalance(@PathVariable String accountId) {
        return accountService.getBalance(accountId);
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you secure Actuator endpoints? Use Spring Security to require authentication for sensitive endpoints, or rely on network-level security by running Actuator on an internal port that is not exposed through the load balancer.
- Can you create a composite health indicator? Yes. Use `CompositeHealthContributor` to group related health checks under a single name.
- What metrics does Spring Boot auto-configure? JVM metrics (memory, GC, threads), HTTP server metrics (request count, duration, status codes), connection pool metrics (HikariCP active/idle connections), and cache metrics.
- How does Prometheus scraping work? Prometheus periodically sends GET requests to `/actuator/prometheus` and stores the time-series data. Grafana queries Prometheus to render dashboards and trigger alerts.
